#Game State

The game state holds everything that changes during play: where the chompers
are (the player marking), where the ghosts are (the ghost marking), where the
dots still are (the dot marking), the score, and the number of lives remaining.

Chompers, ghosts and dots are all Petri net tokens over the one net, tracked in
separate markings. They differ in what changes them: a chomper moves when the
player fires a transition, a ghost when a ghost turn does, and a dot never
moves at all — it is consumed when a chomper arrives and is not put back when
a life is lost. Keeping them apart keeps the net semantics clean — each
population fires independently — and makes it easy to check collisions (a
place where a chomper and ghost both have tokens is a collision).

A map may declare multiple playerStarts in its #Game Map definition, placing
one chomper token at each start place when the game initialises. All start tokens are also
restored on a life reset. Single-start maps are the common case; multi-start
maps expose fork-join coordination from the very first move.

    interface GameState {
      gn: GameNet;
      chompers: Marking;     // player's tokens
      ghosts: Marking;       // ghost tokens
      dots: Marking;         // dots still to eat, per place
      score: number;
      lives: number;
    }

Chompers start as a mark on a place: playerStarts, with a count from
startMarkings. Ghosts and dots start as the ghostStart and dotStart markings.
Dealing chompers and ghosts is shared between the initial deal and a
post-death reset, so both read the exact same start rules from the #Game Map;
dots are dealt once, since a reset does not restore them.

    function spawnChompers(gn: GameNet): Marking {
      const chompers = emptyMarking();
      for (const startId of gn.playerStarts) {
        markingAddTokens(chompers, startId, gn.startMarkings?.get(startId) ?? 1);
      }
      return chompers;
    }

    function spawnGhosts(gn: GameNet): Marking {
      const ghosts = emptyMarking();
      for (const [placeId, count] of gn.ghostStart) {
        markingAddTokens(ghosts, placeId, count);
      }
      return ghosts;
    }

    function initState(gn: GameNet, lives = 3): GameState {
      const chompers = spawnChompers(gn);
      const ghosts = spawnGhosts(gn);

      const dots = emptyMarking();
      for (const [placeId, count] of gn.dotStart) {
        markingAddTokens(dots, placeId, count);
      }

      return { gn, chompers, ghosts, dots, score: 0, lives };
    }

##Dot Eating and Scoring

When a chomper is in a place that has dots, every dot there is removed and the
score rises by ten for each. This happens as a side-effect of firing a
transition — the arrival place is checked after each fire.

    function eatDots(state: GameState): void {
      for (const [placeId, count] of state.chompers) {
        const here = tokenCount(state.dots, placeId);
        if (count > 0 && here > 0) {
          markingRemoveTokens(state.dots, placeId, here);
          state.score += 10 * here;
        }
      }
    }

##Collision Detection

Chompers and ghosts are two token colours in the same net — a coloured Petri
net in all but name. A collision occurs when they share a place. Rather than
a global reset, the ghost consumes exactly one chomper token from each shared
place. Only when no chomper tokens remain does the player lose a life and all
tokens reset to starting positions.

This makes splitting tactically meaningful: two chompers grant resilience, as
a ghost can only eat one per encounter. It also keeps the token semantics
consistent — removal is always a consumption event.

resolveCollisions returns the place ids where a chomper was just eaten, so
the renderer can flash the spot — the state mutation and the information a
caller needs to animate it happen together, in the one place that knows it.

resetToStart and loseLife are shared with the deadlock check below — losing
a life always means "reset positions," whether a ghost ate your last token
or the net you're standing on simply has nothing left to fire.

    function hasCollision(state: GameState): boolean {
      for (const [placeId, count] of state.chompers) {
        if (count > 0 && (state.ghosts.get(placeId) ?? 0) > 0)
          return true;
      }
      return false;
    }

    function resetToStart(state: GameState): void {
      state.chompers = spawnChompers(state.gn);
      state.ghosts = spawnGhosts(state.gn);
    }

    function loseLife(state: GameState): void {
      state.lives -= 1;
      resetToStart(state);
    }

    function resolveCollisions(state: GameState): string[] {
      const eaten: string[] = [];
      for (const [placeId, count] of state.chompers) {
        if (count > 0 && (state.ghosts.get(placeId) ?? 0) > 0)
          eaten.push(placeId);
      }
      if (eaten.length === 0) return eaten;
      for (const placeId of eaten)
        markingRemoveTokens(state.chompers, placeId, 1);
      const total = [...state.chompers.values()].reduce((a, b) => a + b, 0);
      if (total > 0) return eaten;
      loseLife(state);
      return eaten;
    }

##Deadlock

A deadlock is a Petri-net concept in its own right: a marking with tokens
still in it, but no transition anywhere enabled for any of them. On a map
like Mandala, where a hard two-token join has no slack, losing one half of
a pair can strand the other half forever with nowhere left to fire — exactly
the condition the escape routes exist to prevent, but a design can still
produce one, and a player should never be stuck looking at a frozen board
with no explanation. Treating it the same as a fatal collision — lose a
life, reset to start — keeps it a recoverable setback rather than a silent
hang, and teaches the concept the same way collisions teach "the ghost ate
you": by naming the Petri-net event that just happened.

    function isDeadlocked(state: GameState): boolean {
      if (markingTotal(state.chompers) === 0) return false;
      return enabledTransitions(state.gn.net, state.chompers).length === 0;
    }

##Win and Lose Conditions

The player wins when the dot marking is empty. The player loses when lives
reach zero.

    function isWon(state: GameState): boolean {
      return markingTotal(state.dots) === 0;
    }

    function isLost(state: GameState): boolean {
      return state.lives <= 0;
    }

The examples below use the circuit map as a representative multi-place net.

    const _crossGn = makeCircuitMap();
    const _s0 = initState(_crossGn);

    function _testEatDot(): { dotsGone: boolean; score: number } {
      const st = initState(_crossGn);
      markingAddTokens(st.chompers, 'nw', 1);
      markingRemoveTokens(st.chompers, 'depot', 1);
      eatDots(st);
      return { dotsGone: tokenCount(st.dots, 'nw') === 0, score: st.score };
    }

    function _testCollision(): boolean {
      const sc = initState(_crossGn);
      markingAddTokens(sc.ghosts, 'depot', 1);
      return hasCollision(sc);
    }

    function _testEatOneChomper(): { upperGone: boolean; lowerSurvived: boolean; livesUnchanged: boolean } {
      const st = initState(makeSplitMap());
      st.chompers.clear();
      markingAddTokens(st.chompers, 'upper', 1);
      markingAddTokens(st.chompers, 'lower', 1);
      st.ghosts.clear();
      markingAddTokens(st.ghosts, 'upper', 1);
      resolveCollisions(st);
      return {
        upperGone:      tokenCount(st.chompers, 'upper') === 0,
        lowerSurvived:  tokenCount(st.chompers, 'lower') === 1,
        livesUnchanged: st.lives === 3,
      };
    }

    function _testLastChomperEaten(): { livesLost: boolean; resetToStart: boolean } {
      const st = initState(_crossGn);
      st.ghosts.clear();
      markingAddTokens(st.ghosts, 'depot', 1);
      resolveCollisions(st);
      return {
        livesLost:    st.lives === 2,
        resetToStart: tokenCount(st.chompers, 'depot') === 1,
      };
    }

A lone token at the Mandala map's west collector is a reachable deadlock:
t_join_w produces it by consuming both nw_c and sw_c, so getting there means
nothing is left on the west arm to retreat to — and t_join needs a token in
c_e too, which this scenario doesn't have. c_w has no escape route of its
own, unlike the corridor places.

    function _testDeadlock(): boolean {
      const st = initState(makeMandalaMap());
      st.chompers.clear();
      markingAddTokens(st.chompers, 'c_w', 1);
      return isDeadlocked(st);
    }

    function _testDeadlockRecovery(): {
      livesLost: boolean; resetToStart: boolean;
    } {
      const st = initState(makeMandalaMap());
      st.chompers.clear();
      markingAddTokens(st.chompers, 'c_w', 1);
      loseLife(st);
      return {
        livesLost:    st.lives === 2,
        resetToStart: tokenCount(st.chompers, 'hub') === 1,
      };
    }

~example
    tokenCount(_s0.chompers, 'depot') === 1
    !isDeadlocked(_s0)
    tokenCount(_s0.dots, 'nw') === 1
    tokenCount(_s0.dots, 'nn') === 1
    tokenCount(_s0.dots, 'ne') === 1
    tokenCount(_s0.dots, 'sw') === 1
    tokenCount(_s0.dots, 'depot') === 0
    _s0.score === 0
    _s0.lives === 3
    !isWon(_s0)
    !isLost(_s0)

---

#Tests

##eating a dot increases score and removes dot
    _testEatDot().dotsGone === true
    _testEatDot().score === 10

##collision detected when chomper and ghost share a place
    _testCollision() === true

##no collision when they differ
    hasCollision(_s0) === false

##split: ghost eats one chomper, other survives, no life lost
    _testEatOneChomper().upperGone
    _testEatOneChomper().lowerSurvived
    _testEatOneChomper().livesUnchanged

##last chomper eaten loses a life and resets to start
    _testLastChomperEaten().livesLost
    _testLastChomperEaten().resetToStart

##a lone token at the west collector is deadlocked
    _testDeadlock()

##deadlock recovery loses a life and resets to start
    _testDeadlockRecovery().livesLost
    _testDeadlockRecovery().resetToStart

